Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:03.25,Title,,0,0,0,,{\fad(700,500)\bord3\blur0.5}ODYSSEY — BOOK I
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.25,0:00:06.30,Deck,,0,0,0,,{\fad(500,700)}Athena comes to Ithaca;\NTelemachus resolves to seek his father.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.30,0:01:38.00,Header,,0,0,0,,{\fad(800,500)}HOMER’S ODYSSEY  ·  BOOK I
Dialogue: 1,0:00:06.30,0:00:09.65,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}Tell{\c&H00D8E7EE&} me the man, Muse —\Nthe man of many turnings,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:09.65,0:00:14.85,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}who{\c&H00D8E7EE&} was driven wandering far, once he had sacked\NTroy’s holy citadel:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:14.85,0:00:18.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}many{\c&H00D8E7EE&} were the cities of men he saw,\Nand he learned their minds,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:18.40,0:00:21.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}many{\c&H00D8E7EE&} the griefs he suffered at heart\Non the open sea,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:21.10,0:00:25.45,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}striving{\c&H00D8E7EE&} to win his own life\Nand his companions’ homecoming.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:25.45,0:00:28.85,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}Even{\c&H00D8E7EE&} so he could not save them,\Nhard though he strove:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:28.85,0:00:31.05,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}they{\c&H00D8E7EE&} perished by their own recklessness,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:31.05,0:00:34.75,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}fools,{\c&H00D8E7EE&} who ate the cattle\Nof Helios Hyperion,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:34.75,0:00:37.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}and{\c&H00D8E7EE&} the god took from them\Ntheir day of homecoming.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:37.50,0:00:41.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}Begin{\c&H00D8E7EE&} where you will, goddess, daughter of Zeus —\Ntell us, too, the tale.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:41.70,0:00:45.35,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}By{\c&H00D8E7EE&} then all the others,\Nall who had escaped sheer ruin,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:45.35,0:00:47.55,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}were{\c&H00D8E7EE&} home, safely past the war\Nand past the sea.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:47.55,0:00:51.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}Him{\c&H00D8E7EE&} alone — aching for homecoming\Nand his wife —
Dialogue: 1,0:00:51.40,0:00:55.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}the{\c&H00D8E7EE&} queenly nymph Calypso,\Nshining among goddesses, held back
Dialogue: 1,0:00:55.10,0:00:58.65,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}in{\c&H00D8E7EE&} her hollow caves,\Nhungry to make him her husband.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:58.65,0:01:01.85,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}But{\c&H00D8E7EE&} when the year came,\Nin the wheeling round of seasons,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:01.85,0:01:05.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}in{\c&H00D8E7EE&} which the gods had spun the thread\Nof his return home to Ithaca —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:05.50,0:01:09.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}not{\c&H00D8E7EE&} even there was he free of trials,\Neven among his own.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:09.70,0:01:14.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}And{\c&H00D8E7EE&} the gods pitied him, all but Poseidon:\Nhe raged without rest
Dialogue: 1,0:01:14.80,0:01:19.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}against{\c&H00D8E7EE&} godlike Odysseus,\Nuntil the day he reached his own land.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:19.80,0:01:22.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}But{\c&H00D8E7EE&} now Poseidon was gone\Namong the far-off Ethiopians —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:22.70,0:01:27.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}the{\c&H00D8E7EE&} Ethiopians, sundered in two,\Nremotest of mankind,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:27.00,0:01:29.45,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}half{\c&H00D8E7EE&} where Hyperion sets\Nand half where he rises —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:29.45,0:01:34.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}to{\c&H00D8E7EE&} receive a hecatomb of bulls and rams,\Nand there he sat at the feast, delighting.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:34.20,0:01:38.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}{\c&H006AB3D7&}But{\c&H00D8E7EE&} the other gods were gathered together\Nin the halls of Olympian Zeus,
